language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ csplib_032_max_density_still_life: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Find the most densely populated still life on an n x m active grid, that is a stable pattern of live and dead cells that does not change from one generation to the next in Conway's Game of Life, and maximise the number of live cells in the grid. Cells outside the active grid are dead, but they are still part of the board. A live cell must have exactly 2 or 3 live neighbours to remain alive, and a dead cell, including a dead cell just outside the active grid, must not have exactly 3 live neighbours, otherwise it would become alive.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `csplib_032_max_density_still_life.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/csplib_032_max_density_still_life.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `csplib_032_max_density_still_life_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py csplib_032_max_density_still_life.json
$   savilerow model.eprime csplib_032_max_density_still_life_1.param -run-solver

$ n: number of rows of the active grid
given n : int(1..)
$ m: number of columns of the active grid
given m : int(1..)

$ P[r,c]: cell (r,c) of the board is live. The active grid is rows 2..n+1 and columns 2..m+1; the two rings of cells around it are dead, so every neighbour lookup is in range
find P : matrix indexed by [int(0..n+3), int(0..m+3)] of bool
$ grid[i,j]: cell (i,j) of the active grid is live (indices 1-based)
find grid : matrix indexed by [int(1..n), int(1..m)] of bool
$ nb[r,c]: number of live neighbours of board cell (r,c), for the cells rows 1..n+2 and columns 1..m+2 (auxiliary, a function of P)
find nb : matrix indexed by [int(1..n+2), int(1..m+2)] of int(0..8)

branching on [grid]
maximising (sum i : int(1..n) . sum j : int(1..m) . toInt(grid[i,j]))
such that
$ the cells outside the active grid are dead: the two rings around it
forAll c : int(0..m+3) . (!P[0,c] /\ !P[1,c] /\ !P[n+2,c] /\ !P[n+3,c]),
forAll r : int(2..n+1) . (!P[r,0] /\ !P[r,1] /\ !P[r,m+2] /\ !P[r,m+3]),
$ the active grid is the central part of the board
forAll i : int(1..n) . forAll j : int(1..m) . (grid[i,j] = P[i+1,j+1]),
$ nb counts the live cells among the eight neighbours of each cell
forAll r : int(1..n+2) . forAll c : int(1..m+2) . (nb[r,c] = (sum a : int(-1..1) . sum b : int(-1..1) . toInt((a != 0 \/ b != 0) /\ P[r+a,c+b]))),
$ a live cell has exactly 2 or 3 live neighbours, so it stays alive
forAll r : int(1..n+2) . forAll c : int(1..m+2) . (P[r,c] -> (nb[r,c] = 2 \/ nb[r,c] = 3)),
$ a dead cell does not have exactly 3 live neighbours, so it does not become alive
forAll r : int(1..n+2) . forAll c : int(1..m+2) . ((!P[r,c]) -> (nb[r,c] != 3))
